  (:adr/body "# ADR 2799007543: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 7543 Product Graders and Testers (excluding Foods and Beverages) sample-intake-logging/session-scheduling coordination coverage

## Status

Accepted

## Context

This is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the
`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing
ADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an
autonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a
`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a
robotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/
administrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that
gates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates
high-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 7543
-- Product Graders and Testers (excluding Foods and Beverages)** only,
Wave3 (production/trades) of the rollout, continuing the batch that
already landed 60 occupations across the 71xx/72xx/73xx/74xx/75xx
trades (Wave0/1/2 100% complete).

The `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"7543\" ...}` entry
was verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M
-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-
occupation \\\"7543\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/
technology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative
path), and its `:name` read exactly \"Product Graders and Testers
(excluding Foods and Beverages)\" -- confirming the premise, no
mismatch. Prior to this ADR the entry's `:maturity` was `:spec` with
`:required-technologies [:robotics :identity :audit-ledger]` and no
`:repo`/`:business-id`; no repository existed at
`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7543` (`gh api` 404 confirmed before
scaffolding). This ADR and its companion registry edit promote the
`7543` entry to `:implemented`.

Product Graders and Testers perform quality-grading/testing evaluation
of manufactured (non-food) products -- this is fundamentally a
quality-decision occupation, genuinely different from most Wave3
trades: its core function IS making a pass/fail/grade judgment, so
this actor cannot simply \"coordinate, never grade\" as a throwaway
phrase -- grading/testing is the occupation's entire purpose. This is
the SAME shape of problem already resolved for the closely analogous,
already-landed `cloud-itonami-isco-7515` (Food and Beverage Tasters
and Graders): the resolution adopted there, and adopted again here, is
that this actor coordinates SAMPLE-INTAKE LOGGING AND SCHEDULING ONLY
-- it NEVER performs the grading/testing evaluation itself, NEVER
records or proposes a grade/pass-fail determination (that requires a
human grader/tester's judgment, always), and NEVER finalizes a
quality-clearance decision. The closed op allowlist is limited to
purely administrative/logistics functions around the human
grader/tester's work -- sample intake logging, scheduling testing
sessions, and flagging anomalies for the human's attention -- never
anything resembling the grading/testing judgment itself.

`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7515` was cloned and read in full in
this session as the primary structural reference: it already
establishes exactly this zero-sensory/grading-judgment-authority
pattern (closed-allowlist + independently-verified-provenance +
always-escalate-concern + content-based scope-exclusion shape) for a
closely analogous quality-decision occupation, so this ADR generalizes
its food-specific framing (sample-intake, tasting-session, taster,
sensory evaluation, food-safety clearance) to non-food product grading/
testing (sample-intake, testing-session, grader/tester, quality
evaluation, quality clearance) rather than re-deriving the pattern from
a more distant reference such as `cloud-itonami-isco-7412` (Electrical
Mechanics and Fitters).

## Decision

Publish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7543` as an OSS sample-
intake-logging/session-scheduling coordination actor for product
graders and testers (excluding foods and beverages), following the
SAME governed-actor architecture as every prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*`
actor in this fleet: **Product Grader/Tester Advisor asymmetric-gated
by ProdGradeGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph` StateGraph
(`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned identically to the
reference's `deps.edn` -- a different library coordinate from the
`langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated `20-actors/*` actor family), with
an independent Governor, human-in-the-loop approval via
`interrupt-before`, and an append-only audit ledger.

This actor is deliberately **sample-intake logging and session
scheduling coordination ONLY, never grading/testing-evaluation or
quality-judgment authority** -- it must NEVER be designed to exercise,
simulate exercising, or propose exercising the grading/testing
evaluation itself, a quality-grade assignment, a pass/fail
determination, or a quality-clearance decision. These are not merely
gated behind escalation -- they are structurally absent from the
op-allowlist entirely.

### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)

- `:log-sample-record` -- sample intake/batch-identifier metadata
  entry (documentation only -- item, timestamp, batch ID -- NEVER a
  grade/pass-fail outcome)
- `:schedule-testing-session` -- human-grader/tester session scheduling
  proposal
- `:flag-anomaly-concern` -- surface a sample-condition anomaly (e.g.
  visible defect reported by intake staff) for the human
  grader/tester's attention, ALWAYS escalates, NEVER auto-commit-
  eligible
- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- testing-session supplies (test
  equipment consumables, sample containers) procurement proposal

This actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles**
performing the grading/testing evaluation itself, assigning or
finalizing a quality-grade, determining or finalizing a pass/fail
outcome, or finalizing or authorizing a quality clearance. These are
**structurally absent from the op-allowlist entirely**, not merely
gated behind escalation -- an op such as `:assign-quality-grade`
cannot even be proposed by the reference mock advisor (its
`infer`/`rationale-for` functions only ever emit one of the four
allowlist ops), and if any code path (a forged proposal, a future LLM
advisor, a misbehaving fork) ever produced such an op, the governor's
closed-allowlist HARD check (`:unknown-op`) rejects it unconditionally
and permanently. A second, independent HARD check
(`:scope-excluded-action`) names eight concretely-forbidden ops across
four scope-exclusion categories (`prodgrade.governor/scope-excluded-
ops`: `:perform-quality-evaluation`, `:conduct-quality-evaluation`,
`:assign-quality-grade`, `:finalize-quality-grade-assignment`,
`:determine-pass-fail-outcome`, `:finalize-pass-fail-determination`,
`:finalize-quality-clearance`, `:authorize-quality-clearance`) as
defense-in-depth, and is verified by eight dedicated unit tests
(`prodgrade.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-perform-quality-
evaluation`, `.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-conduct-quality-
evaluation`, `.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-assign-quality-grade`,
`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-quality-grade-assignment`,
`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-determine-pass-fail-outcome`,
`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-pass-fail-determination`,
`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-quality-clearance`,
`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-quality-clearance`).

### Four HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)

1. **Grader/tester unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an
   independently registered grader record, including certification
   status (`:no-grader`). Never trusts the proposal's own claim of who
   the grader/tester is.
2. **Facility unregistered** -- any proposal must cite an
   independently registered facility (`:no-facility`). Together with
   check 1, this directly implements the CRITICAL invariant that a
   grader/tester/facility record must be independently verified/
   registered before any action.
3. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD
   block (a claim to directly actuate outside governance)
   (`:no-actuation`).
4. **Op not allowed / scope-excluded action** -- any `:op` outside the
   closed four-op allowlist is a HARD, PERMANENT block (`:unknown-op`),
   and independently, any proposal whose `:op` names one of the eight
   concretely-forbidden ops above, OR whose free-text `:rationale`
   names a finalization/execution action for performing the grading/
   testing evaluation, assigning or finalizing a quality grade,
   determining or finalizing a pass/fail outcome, or finalizing or
   authorizing a quality clearance, is a second HARD, PERMANENT block
   (`:scope-excluded-action`), evaluated unconditionally on content --
   defense-in-depth on top of the allowlist check: even if a future
   change somehow widened the allowlist, content-based rejection still
   independently blocks grading/testing-evaluation/quality-grade/
   pass-fail/quality-clearance language wherever it appears in free
   text.

### One always-escalate (SOFT) gate, one threshold gate, plus the general confidence floor

- `:flag-anomaly-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless
  of confidence (governor's implicit always-risky check). This is the
  ONLY channel by which the robot may surface a sample-condition
  anomaly observation -- it never resolves the concern itself, and the
  robot's role ends at \"here is the anomaly data,\" never \"here is
  what I recommend doing about this sample's quality.\"
- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds
  `supply-cost-threshold` (2000, inclusive boundary verified by
  `ok-supply-order-at-threshold-boundary`) -- always needs human
  sign-off, independent of confidence. This is an ESCALATION, not a
  hard block, per the task's explicit domain design: an
  over-threshold testing-supply order is not itself a quality-
  clearance determination, unlike a grading/testing-evaluation,
  quality-grade, pass-fail-determination, or quality-clearance
  attempt, which is why it is handled by a different mechanism (soft
  escalate vs. structural absence).
- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for
  any op.)

### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, carried over from the sibling ISIC/ISCO tracks, including 7515's own precedent)

A governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.
\"quality\", \"grade\", \"test\", \"clearance\") can accidentally match
inside the mock advisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate
proposal, self-blocking the actor's own happy path -- this is exactly
the risk this vertical's own vocabulary (a sample-intake/scheduling
coordination actor working adjacent to live product-quality work and
human graders/testers necessarily discusses those concepts to do its
scheduling/logging job) could trigger if phrased carelessly.
`prodgrade.governor/scope-excluded-phrases` is phrased exclusively as
finalization/execution ACTIONS across all four scope-exclusion
categories (e.g. \"perform the quality evaluation\", \"assign the
quality grade\", \"determine the pass/fail outcome\", \"finalize the
quality clearance\" -- matching the task's own worked example phrasing
verbatim), never bare nouns, and `prodgrade.governor-test/default-
mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-on-scope-exclusion` is a
dedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed allowlist,
driven through the default mock advisor -- including a deliberately
loaded request containing the bare nouns \"quality-grade\" and
\"anomaly\"/\"defect\" in its `:item` and `:anomaly-type` fields --
never trips `:scope-excluded-action`. The mock advisor's own default
rationale template was deliberately written to contain the bare nouns
(\"logged sample intake record for grader ...\", \"scheduled testing
session for quality evaluation panel ...\", \"...routed for the human
grader/tester's attention\") WITHOUT containing any of the full
finalization-action phrases, so the regression test is a genuine
stress test of the bare-noun-vs-action-phrase distinction, not a
vacuous pass. Green on first run in this session -- the scope-
exclusion self-trip guard itself was never tripped during development.
`prodgrade.actor-test/holds-a-scope-excluded-op-even-at-high-
confidence`, `.../holds-a-pass-fail-determination-op-even-at-high-
confidence`, `.../holds-a-quality-clearance-op-even-at-high-
confidence` and `.../holds-a-perform-quality-evaluation-op-even-at-
high-confidence` additionally exercise the same invariant through the
full graph run for each of the four scope-exclusion categories,
confirming a scope-excluded proposal never reaches `commit-record!`
regardless of disposition path.

## Consequences

- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"7543\" ...}` entry
  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`
  set to the real repository
  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7543` /
  `cloud-itonami-isco-7543`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented
  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via
  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately
  before the edit). A dedicated per-occupation `deftest` (race-safe
  convention for this hot-contention file, several sibling agents
  landing concurrently this batch) was added asserting `7543`'s
  maturity/repo/business-id, rather than touching the fragile
  aggregate `maturity-tier` deftest.
- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 29 tests, 64 assertions, 0
  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's
  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified
  against a brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just
  the local working copy). No mojibake found in any committed source
  file.
- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via
  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with
  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.
- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's
  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry
  itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies` stacks.
- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention
  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to
  tx-data\") -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string
  blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.

## Alternatives considered

- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-quality-grade` or
  `:approve-quality-clearance` that is merely gated behind an
  always-escalate list (the shape some earlier actors in this fleet
  use for threshold-gated approvals): rejected outright per the task's
  explicit domain-design guidance -- an always-escalate op still means
  the *capability* to finalize a quality-grade/pass-fail/quality-
  clearance decision exists in the code path and is merely deferred to
  a human gate. For a decision this occupation-defining (the human
  grader/tester's judgment is the entire purpose of the occupation and
  is irreplaceable), the capability itself must not exist. The only op
  related to quality escalation is `:flag-anomaly-concern`, which
  surfaces the need for the human grader/tester's attention and never
  itself decides, grades, or defers a quality decision for later
  automatic execution.
- Making the supply-cost ceiling a HARD block (mirroring some earlier
  actors' transaction-amount-ceiling HARD checks): rejected per the
  task's explicit domain design -- an over-threshold testing-supply
  order is not itself a quality-clearance determination, so it is
  handled as a soft escalate gate, not a hard block; only the grading/
  testing-evaluation/quality-grade/pass-fail-determination/quality-
  clearance classes are a hard, unconditional block.
- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"quality\" \"grade\"
  \"test\" \"clearance\"]`): rejected outright per the fleet-known
  self-trip bug class described above (and CLAUDE.md's own worked
  example for this exact task), and per the explicit instruction to
  phrase scope-exclusion terms as finalization/execution ACTIONS,
  never bare nouns -- this actor's own domain necessarily discusses
  quality/grade/test/clearance concepts to do its scheduling/logging
  job, so a bare-noun list would risk self-blocking the actor's own
  legitimate proposals (regression-tested directly against rationale/
  request text that deliberately contains those bare nouns).
- Collapsing the four scope-exclusion categories (quality-evaluation-
  performance, quality-grade-assignment, pass-fail-determination,
  quality-clearance) into a single generic \"quality-judgment\"
  op/category: rejected -- CLAUDE.md's CRITICAL section names these as
  textually distinct finalization/execution actions (\"assign the
  quality grade\", \"determine the pass/fail outcome\", \"finalize the
  quality clearance\"), and collapsing them would blur the
  defense-in-depth op-naming and phrase-matching granularity that
  makes the self-trip regression test a genuine stress test rather
  than a coincidence; each category also has a semantically distinct
  real-world referent (physically inspecting/measuring a sample vs.
  assigning a grade label vs. deciding pass/fail vs. clearing a batch
  for quality release) worth naming and testing independently.
- Merging `grader` and `facility` into a single entity (only checking
  one provenance record): rejected -- the two-entity provenance shape
  (independently-registered grader/tester AND independently-registered
  facility) matches the CRITICAL invariant that a grader/tester/
  facility record must be independently verified/registered before any
  action and the established pattern from prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*`
  actors, including 7515's own `taster`/`facility` split, with
  two-sided provenance checks.

## Itemized safety confirmation

Every op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is
safe under the constraints above:

1. **`:log-sample-record`** -- sample intake/batch-identifier metadata
   entry only (item, timestamp, batch ID). Requires an independently
   registered grader/tester AND facility (`:no-grader`/`:no-facility`).
   Not a grading/testing evaluation, quality-grade, pass-fail-
   determination, or quality-clearance decision in any respect, and
   NEVER carries a grade/pass-fail outcome field -- no such field
   exists anywhere in this proposal's shape. It is a
   records-management action.
2. **`:schedule-testing-session`** -- human-grader/tester session
   scheduling proposal only. Cannot carry a quality-grade/pass-fail
   decision (structurally absent from the op's field shape; any such
   attempt via free text is caught by the `:scope-excluded-action`
   content check). Does not authorize, finalize, or draw any
   conclusion about a specific sample's quality or clearance status --
   it is a calendar/logistics action.
3. **`:flag-anomaly-concern`** -- surfaces a sample-condition anomaly
   (visible defect reported by intake staff) to the human
   grader/tester; ALWAYS escalates, unconditionally, at every
   confidence level, NEVER auto-commit eligible. This is the ONLY
   channel through which a condition observation may be surfaced, and
   it explicitly never resolves, decides, grades, or acts on anything
   itself -- the human grader/tester reviews and judges entirely on
   their own authority.
4. **`:coordinate-supply-order`** -- testing-session supplies (test
   equipment consumables, sample containers) procurement proposal only
   (item, cost). Not a grading/testing evaluation, quality-grade,
   pass-fail-determination, or quality-clearance decision of any kind
   -- it is a purchasing-logistics action, gated by ordinary
   cost-ceiling escalation (above 2000 always escalates).

No op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase, resembles
performing the grading/testing evaluation itself, assigning or
finalizing a quality-grade assignment, determining or finalizing a
pass/fail outcome, or finalizing or authorizing a quality clearance --
these categories are structurally absent, not gated, confirmed by: (a)
the closed four-op allowlist itself (`prodgrade.governor/allowed-
ops`), (b) eight concretely-named forbidden-op fixtures rejected at
the governor-unit level (`prodgrade.governor/scope-excluded-ops`,
`prodgrade.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-perform-quality-
evaluation`, `.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-conduct-quality-
evaluation`, `.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-assign-quality-grade`,
`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-quality-grade-assignment`,
`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-determine-pass-fail-outcome`,
`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-pass-fail-determination`,
`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-quality-clearance`,
`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-quality-clearance`), and (c)
a content-based scope-exclusion check independent of the `:op` field
(`prodgrade.governor/scope-excluded-phrases`,
`.../contains-excluded-phrase?`), tested directly against rationale
text that attempts to assign the quality grade, determine the
pass/fail outcome, or finalize the quality clearance even via an
otherwise-allowlisted op (`prodgrade.governor-test/hard-on-scope-
excluded-rationale-assign-the-quality-grade-even-with-allowlisted-op`,
`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-determine-the-pass-fail-
outcome-even-with-allowlisted-op`, `.../hard-on-scope-excluded-
rationale-finalize-the-quality-clearance`). The human grader/tester's
judgment is irreplaceable and this actor never touches it, at any
layer, in any disposition path.")))
